WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.101
-My first guest tonight

00:00:02.101 --> 00:00:04.337
is an Emmy- and
Golden Globe-winning actress.

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:07.607
She stars in and executive
produces "The Morning Show."

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:11.611
Season two of the show is out on
Apple TV+ starting today.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:14.814
Please welcome Jennifer Aniston!

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:16.049
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:17.617
Yeah!
That's what I'm talking about!

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:19.219
Ha ha!

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:23.823
Ohh, man!
Come on. Now, look at --

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:27.059
Jennifer, you're getting --
-Hi, everybody.

00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:28.661
-You're getting, like,
a standing ovation

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:30.629
through the Zoom.
-I am?

00:00:30.629 --> 00:00:33.065
-Yes, people love you!
We love you.

00:00:33.065 --> 00:00:35.201
Thank you for coming back.
Please.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.438
-I so wish I was there in that
room with you right now.

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:40.573
I can't tell you.
I miss New York.

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:44.177
I miss your face.
-Oh, good. We miss you.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:46.613
New York City misses you.
I know you're from here.

00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:49.381
You grew up here.
-Yeah.

00:00:49.381 --> 00:00:54.287
-Did you always want to be
an actress as a kid?

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:57.857
-Pretty much.
Yeah, I think I was --

00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:00.226
You know, I did plays --
little plays.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.262
I went to a Waldorf school,
Rudolf Steiner School

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:06.399
on 79th Street.
And, yeah, I was very --

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.768
And my dad obviously is an
actor, still, on a soap opera.

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.236
-Yeah, of course.

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:12.338
-So yeah, pretty young
I got that bug.

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:16.776
-Yeah. And when did you feel
like you finally made it?

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:18.811
-Oh, when did I finally make it?

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:21.814
Well, you know,
Bob's Big Boy commercial.

00:01:21.814 --> 00:01:23.182
-Wow.
-That's when I finally --

00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:24.617
[ Laughter ]
But you have to understand,

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:28.087
I auditioned a lot,
and I never --

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:30.356
I just couldn't get arrested.

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:33.793
And it was Bob's Big Boy.
That was my first job.

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:36.195
And I thought,
"Wow, I have arrived."

00:01:36.195 --> 00:01:38.898
-Were you --
Are you good at auditions?

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:40.767
-No!
-I'm terrible.

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.234
-Terrible. Awful.
-I am -- I did one

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:44.036
commercial audition once
where I was supposed to be

00:01:44.036 --> 00:01:46.306
a bike messenger.
And I showed up

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:49.976
for the audition, and everyone
was dressed as a bike messenger.

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:53.746
And I had no -- I had like two
outfits in my whole closet.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:56.115
I'm like, "You have an extra
bike messenger outfit?"

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:57.583
Like, what?
-Yeah.

00:01:57.583 --> 00:01:58.917
-Right before I was supposed
to do it, he goes,

00:01:58.917 --> 00:01:59.952
"You're out of breath.

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:01.888
You have to deliver this
whatever it was."

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:03.823
And I go, "Uh-huh."
And he goes, "So you're sweaty."

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:05.258
And he took out a water bottle

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:06.759
and he sprayed me in the face
with water.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:10.029
[ Laughter ]
-Well, look at you now.

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:11.797
-That's exactly --
Look at us now.

00:02:11.797 --> 00:02:13.065
Jinx!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:13.065 --> 00:02:15.968
Jinx.
-Jinx.

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:18.671
-From that to winning an Emmy
on "Friends."

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:21.373
And, also, speaking of Emmys,
this weekend is the Emmys,

00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:23.309
and you're nominated
for "Friends" again.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.011
Isn't that interesting?
For the reunion.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.113
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I know.

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:29.882
-That's kind of cool.
-17 years -- 17 years later.

00:02:29.882 --> 00:02:31.651
Isn't that crazy?
-I loved it.

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:33.419
-It's so wild.
-I loved the reunion.

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:35.021
I thought it was cool.
I just thought that --

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:36.722
I loved that everyone
got together.

00:02:36.722 --> 00:02:38.124
I was so emotional.
-Thanks.

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:40.025
-I could just imagine
what you guys were like.

00:02:40.025 --> 00:02:41.794
But I was watching like -- whew!
-Yeah.

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:45.398
-Every time everyone would bring
up the thing, I would get all --

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:47.299
Just watching it
was just emotional and great.

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:49.068
I loved it.
-It was emotional.

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.903
-Thanks for doing that.
-It really was.

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:52.638
We did it for you, Jimmy.
It was --

00:02:52.638 --> 00:02:54.941
-I know. Thank you.
I appreciate that.

00:02:54.941 --> 00:02:56.542
Oh, you know what was --
-You're welcome.

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:59.345
-What was great, right after
that came out for about --

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.247
maybe about eight hours,
there was a rumor

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:03.048
that you and David Schwimmer
were dating.

00:03:03.048 --> 00:03:06.786
And I thought that was awesome.
-That was a rumor.

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:10.323
That window -- we shut that
one down pretty quickly.

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:13.092
[ Laughter ]
-The world got excited there.

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:14.693
-They just wanted it so bad.

00:03:14.693 --> 00:03:16.595
They believe
so much in Ross and Rachel.

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:18.698
I mean, didn't you have
all of those --

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:20.233
-Yeah.
-For me, it was like

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:21.768
Luke and Laura
on "General Hospital."

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:23.101
-Ohh!
-Or, you know,

00:03:23.101 --> 00:03:25.204
"Joanie Loves Chachi."
-Oh, imagine if Luke and Laura

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:27.240
really da--
That would be amazing.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.641
I love Luke and Laura.
-I know.

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:30.676
And I --
It made me so sad.

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:34.813
And, you know, when I was a kid,
I would record,

00:03:34.813 --> 00:03:37.550
because we didn't have VCR --
We just didn't have a VCR.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.218
-Yeah.
-"Joanie Loves Chachi"

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:42.055
or "Happy Days"
or all of those shows,

00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:43.823
"Laverne and Shirley,"
on a tape recorder.

00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:45.592
Like a -- Like a --
-Yeah.

00:03:45.592 --> 00:03:47.727
-Like, stick it into a...
-I would do that.

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:49.995
-...tape deck,
and I would hit record.

00:03:49.995 --> 00:03:53.832
And I would listen to the shows
as I went to bed.

00:03:53.832 --> 00:03:55.100
-That's wild.
I would do that

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.737
with "Saturday Night Live."
I would tape it,

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.472
and then I would, like,
listen to them back.

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:02.741
I would lip sync, like,
Steve Martin or Richard Pryor,

00:04:02.741 --> 00:04:04.544
and just do all that
in my bedroom.

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:06.945
That was, like, the thing.
Because we -- there was no VCR.

00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:08.548
There was nothing to tape.
-No.

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:09.816
-That's wild.
-But there you go.

00:04:09.816 --> 00:04:13.086
See? Again, and look at you,
where you ended up.

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:14.654
And look where --
-Look at you. Look at us.

00:04:14.654 --> 00:04:16.122
Come on. Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:18.857
This is all early years.
You know, by the way,

00:04:18.857 --> 00:04:20.893
I was a bike messenger.
When you say you auditioned

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:22.929
for a bike --
Did I ever tell you that?

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:24.596
-You were a bike messenger?

00:04:24.596 --> 00:04:27.533
-I was a bike messenger
for a day in New York City.

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:28.901
[ Laughter ]
-That counts.

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.470
-I worked at this -- I worked
at an advertising agency

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.606
after school.
My mom actually worked there.

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:36.942
So I would, to make extra money,
I would do the odd jobs.

00:04:36.942 --> 00:04:41.614
And then, one day,
their bike messenger was not --

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:42.915
just didn't show up.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.617
And they were like,
"Can you ride a bike?"

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:47.586
And I was like,
"Yeah, I can ride a bike."

00:04:47.586 --> 00:04:49.054
-It's a gig.
-Now, mind you,

00:04:49.054 --> 00:04:50.823
in Central Park,
I can ride a bike.

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.091
I mean...
-On the street...

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:54.593
-...with soft areas to land.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:58.597
But so I just boldly said yes.
And they gave me this bag,

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:01.467
and they put
all these cylinders in this bag.

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:03.635
And, oh, it was just
not a great --

00:05:03.635 --> 00:05:06.338
-Cylinders like Mike Brady?
Like, plans?

00:05:06.338 --> 00:05:08.907
Like, those tubes?
-Yeah, and they were sealed.

00:05:08.907 --> 00:05:10.910
You'd have to deliver
to the wherever.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:14.246
I mean, in my brain,
I don't know how I survived

00:05:14.246 --> 00:05:16.982
the day because it was --
to be on Fifth Avenue

00:05:16.982 --> 00:05:20.052
with traffic
and all that I was holding,

00:05:20.052 --> 00:05:24.190
and then I think I might have
gotten into a cab

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.891
just to finish the day.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.891 --> 00:05:28.027
-You were a bike messenger.
You made sure it got there.

00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:30.562
That's all that matters.
-I just had to get it there.

00:05:30.562 --> 00:05:32.564
So I -- I don't know
what happened to the bike.

00:05:32.564 --> 00:05:34.200
-You delivered.
-It's all a blur.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.501
-I wanted to reach out to you

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.869
because I was watching
"The Morning Show."

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:38.204
I loved it, by the way,
season one.

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:40.006
You were fantastic.
-Thank you.

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:43.009
-Reese Witherspoon is fantastic.
The show's great.

00:05:43.009 --> 00:05:45.011
-Thanks.
-But there was a scene with you

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:48.614
and Billy Crudup
where you were both singing...

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:51.184
♪ No one's gonna harm you,
not while -- ♪

00:05:51.184 --> 00:05:52.651
-Yes!
-I love that scene.

00:05:52.651 --> 00:05:53.686
-Sondheim.

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:55.220
-Dude, that was
my favorite scene.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.356
And it's Broadway week here
at our show.

00:05:57.356 --> 00:06:00.225
Would you ever do
a Broadway show?

00:06:00.225 --> 00:06:03.329
-I would love to do
a Broadway show in my --

00:06:03.329 --> 00:06:05.198
[ Cheers and applause ]
In my head, it sounds like --

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:06.365
What? What?
Really?

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:07.399
-People would like it.
People like it.

00:06:07.399 --> 00:06:08.701
-Really?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:13.005
People like this idea!
We could be onto something here.

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:15.607
-We could be onto something
here, because I would --

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:19.512
In fact, I loved --

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:22.314
Billy and I both took
singing lessons for this

00:06:22.314 --> 00:06:25.084
'cause it -- not only was it
one of the hardest --

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:27.453
Sondheim is like, "Ugh!"
-Yeah.

00:06:27.453 --> 00:06:28.954
-"No One's Going To Harm You,"

00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:33.793
it's not the easiest, lyrically
or musically, to sing.

00:06:33.793 --> 00:06:36.161
So, we had to --
thank God, a professional,

00:06:36.161 --> 00:06:38.530
that we each spent
a couple of days with.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:44.002
And I had so much fun
taking music -- singing lessons.

00:06:44.002 --> 00:06:46.305
-It is fun.
-And so much, in fact,

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:47.907
a friend of mine actually
gifted me

00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:51.643
with 10 singing lessons with
this said singing teacher.

00:06:51.643 --> 00:06:56.282
So, I --
if I can get my act together,

00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:57.917
maybe I would
even be in a musical.

00:06:57.917 --> 00:06:59.085
-Wow!
All right.

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:00.253
Well, this is big.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:02.355
That's a good present.
-This is big.

00:07:02.355 --> 00:07:04.223
I mean,
we're just halfway through,

00:07:04.223 --> 00:07:06.358
so why not start something
completely new?

00:07:06.358 --> 00:07:07.993
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:11.230
-Can we talk about season two
of "The Morning Show"?

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:14.100
What's happening this season
with Alex?

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.769
-Oh, what's not happening, guys?

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:22.675
It's, uh --
It's pretty twisty, turny

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:24.744
couple of 10 episodes
you have ahead of you.

00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:27.847
Well, we start out,
Alex has kind of removed herself

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:32.651
from the chaos that we left
at the end of season one

00:07:32.651 --> 00:07:35.220
to sort of take stock,
inventory,

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.223
on what's it all about.
Alfie.

00:07:38.223 --> 00:07:43.929
And then she gets
sucked back in my Mr. Crudup.

00:07:43.929 --> 00:07:46.465
And, you know, I think she
believes she can take this

00:07:46.465 --> 00:07:52.304
newfound wisdom and, you know,
self-realization

00:07:52.304 --> 00:07:56.308
that she's gained with her
back into the TMS world,

00:07:56.308 --> 00:07:58.644
but not so easy.
Not so fast.

00:07:58.644 --> 00:08:00.512
-No.
-Each character goes through

00:08:00.512 --> 00:08:05.717
some incredible twists and turns
and unexpected things.

00:08:05.717 --> 00:08:08.821
Unimaginable things happen.
-Yeah.

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:11.056
You tackle some serious issues
in this show,

00:08:11.056 --> 00:08:13.559
but I will say it's also
very humorous, as well.

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:15.528
There's funny parts of it.
-Yes.

00:08:15.528 --> 00:08:17.496
-And there's relatable parts.
'Cause it's a morning show.

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:18.730
-Yes.
-So, anyways --

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:20.032
-Yeah.
Well, I mean, it's fun.

00:08:20.032 --> 00:08:22.334
-Exactly.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:22.334 --> 00:08:24.403
Here's Jennifer Aniston
in "The Morning Show."

00:08:24.403 --> 00:08:26.605
Take a look at this.

00:08:26.605 --> 00:08:28.708
-I can't come back.

00:08:28.708 --> 00:08:31.711
Even if I wanted to come back,
I just couldn't.

00:08:31.711 --> 00:08:34.446
I-I --
I would look flaky.

00:08:34.446 --> 00:08:36.048
Wishy-washy.
-No, no, no, no.

00:08:36.048 --> 00:08:37.516
When your new deal
got announced,

00:08:37.516 --> 00:08:39.051
you looked smart as...

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:45.758 line:0%
♪♪

00:08:45.758 --> 00:08:47.726
-I don't want to.
I don't want to.

00:08:47.726 --> 00:08:50.329
I'm done.
I'm done.

00:08:50.329 --> 00:08:51.930
You know what, Cory?
I think it's --

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:53.832
I think you better go.

00:08:53.832 --> 00:08:55.568
I mean,
you're not a horrible person.

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:58.738
I thoroughly enjoy you.

00:08:58.738 --> 00:09:00.672
I mean, maybe you are actually
a horrible person,

00:09:00.672 --> 00:09:02.875
but I still find you
entertaining.

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:04.210
You're a little --

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:08.981
You're a little crazy,
and it...scares me.

00:09:08.981 --> 00:09:10.783
-Yeah! Come on!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:10.783 --> 00:09:14.186
You guys are fantastic together!
It is so good.

00:09:14.186 --> 00:09:16.289
Jennifer Aniston, everybody!

00:09:16.289 --> 00:09:18.591
The new season
of "The Morning Show"

00:09:18.591 --> 00:09:21.527
is out on Apple TV+
starting today.

